From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] server: release the cursor clip when the foreground thread ends

Purpose: the desktop keeps taking clicks after a program that held the mouse
has ended.
Windows behavior: a program that ends does not leave the cursor clipped (not
measured here on a real XP; the probe runs there).
Wine before: thread_input_destroy() only forgot the foreground input. Unlike
set_foreground_input() it did not reset the clip rectangle, so nobody was told
to release it: GetClipCursor() kept the dead program's rectangle until the
foreground changed again, and winex11's clipping window, which belongs to the
desktop process, stayed mapped. It is an invisible InputOnly window above the
desktop; once the X server has dropped the dead process's pointer grab, every
click on it is lost. In the XP session no desktop icon took a click until a
Wine window became the foreground.
Change: when the foreground input is destroyed, reset the clip as
set_foreground_input() does; the desktop thread then unmaps the window.
Evidence: tests/clip-exit.c, with a child that is killed and one that exits:
before, its 200x200 rectangle stayed and so did the mapped clipping window
(32-bit); after, the whole screen again (32-bit and 64-bit) and no window. On
the EeePC a 1024x600 clipping window of explorer.exe /desktop was found above
XP's desktop.
Scope: all programs; only when the foreground thread's input goes away.
---
 server/queue.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/server/queue.c b/server/queue.c
index 3e8ab2e..bf41921 100644
--- a/server/queue.c
+++ b/server/queue.c
@@ -1374,7 +1374,14 @@ static void thread_input_destroy( struct object *obj )
     empty_msg_list( &input->msg_list );
     if ((desktop = input->desktop))
     {
-        if (desktop->foreground_input == input) desktop->foreground_input = NULL;
+        if (desktop->foreground_input == input)
+        {
+            /* The foreground thread ended without handing the foreground on, so
+             * nobody else releases its cursor clip: do it as set_foreground_input
+             * does, which also tells the desktop thread to undo the driver's grab. */
+            desktop->foreground_input = NULL;
+            set_clip_rectangle( desktop, NULL, SET_CURSOR_NOCLIP, 1 );
+        }
         release_object( desktop );
     }
     if (input->shared) free_shared_object( input->shared );
